## Activity

Leader: For this game you need a large, open space. If you go outside, make sure there are clear boundaries. If your space is too small or your group too large, it’s possible to play two or more rounds with just part of the group playing each time.

This is a game of linking. You will move through this space, and when you touch someone else— intentionally or not—you are connected to them. You will then link arms and move together. If you touch someone else, you will add them to the group, and so on. You can add a pair of linked people to your group if you touch either one, but a group of three is “safe”—it cannot be taken over by another group.

There are two ways to win this game. One is to be the last unconnected person in the game. The other is to be a part of the largest group in the game. So, once you do link up, you want to get as many others in your group as possible. I will randomly connect two people to start the game.

**Leader**: By starting with a random connection, you assure that there will be some movement. As people escape from the growing group, they will connect with others (perhaps accidentally) and the game will go on. You may want to announce when a group gets three people and is “safe.” When there is only one unattached person, the game is over. Count up the number in each group and hail the largest group, as well as the last person standing. If there’s time and interest, you could play again . . . and see if they employ different strategies.

Then talk about it . . .

### What were you trying to do, connect or not connect?

Were you thinking of yourself as an individual or as part of a group?

We learn a little something about life in this game. Our connections—our relationships—identify us. You are labeled as “So-and-so’s friend.” Or “one of that group.” Or “part of that family.” That identity could be good or bad, depending on whom you’re linked with, but it helps to shape who you are, and how you’re seen.

Discussion



Leader: If you choose not to do the activity above, you can start with this discussion. Even if you have done the activity, you can continue to this discussion if you like.



Who are the five people in your life who have influenced you the most?



I don’t need answers right away, but I want you to think about it. Who has made you what you are today? Most of you would need to include your parents, no matter what kind of relationship you have with them, but after them who else? Brothers and sisters? If you have more than one sibling, maybe there’s one who has influenced you more than the others. But apart from family, who else?



Teachers, pastors, youth leaders? Maybe.



Friends? Maybe. Are there some who influence you more than you influence them, or vice versa? Often people are influenced by boyfriends or girlfriends.



So, now that you’ve thought about it, who has influenced you, and how? Is there anyone you want to mention?



Do you think it’s good to be shaped by the people around us, or should we just be our own person?



Yes and yes, we’d have to say, right? It’s great to have an inner sense of who you are, and not to be swayed too much by whomever you’re with. But the fact is, we are influenced by others. We can’t avoid it. In fact, that’s sort of what a relationship is. If other people don’t affect your life, you’re probably not “relating” very well. The result can be good or bad, depending on the quality of those relationships and the people in them.



Today we’re going to focus on relationships, and how they affect our identity.

VIEW
Watch Session 3: Relationships (27 Minutes).

REVIEW
We heard a number of things about the relationships between guys and girls. Do you think it’s better, spiritually speaking, not to have a boyfriend or girlfriend?



Do you think it’s possible for a boyfriend or girlfriend to help you grow closer to God?



Leader: Okay, we’ve jumped right into it. You need to use your best judgment to guide this discussion. Your students expect you to tell them not to have sex, and that’s an important message. We’ve certainly seen several examples in these videos of how sexual relationships led people away from the Lord. But they might need to hear more from you. How can a teenage romance be healthy rather than unhealthy? How can your students shape their identity in good ways through these love-relationships? Obviously, this isn’t the only type of relationship that shapes identities, but we’ll start the discussion there.



In the video we heard first from Katy, whose relationship with her boyfriend got a little too intense. Here’s what she said:



My relationship with him pretty much controlled everything about me. It controlled my mood and how I felt. If we were doing great, it made me happy in that moment . . . I was in a good mood—but if we weren’t doing good (which was most of the time), I was in an awful mood and rude to my family and rude to my friends.



Have you seen that happen—either in your own relationship or as one of those “family or friends”?



Why do we let those relationships control our moods like that? Is there anything we can do about it?



Katy found that this relationship was affecting her relationship with God. She said: I was still trying to pursue the Lord but I couldn’t really do that and pursue my boyfriend at the same time. Is it possible to do both? Could she have a healthy relationship with both God and her boyfriend? How would she do it? (And how could you do that in your relationships?)



Later we met Philip, who got the nickname Player.



Why do you think people called him that?



Think about that for a moment. This became his identity. He was a friendly guy, a joker, good with words. He could have been known for any number of good qualities in his life, but this became his tag. He played people. And maybe some of that came from his easy-going attitude—his whole life was a game he played. But you know what we’re talking about. He said it himself. He played girls. He used them.



And the whole time, he was supposedly a Christian.



So let’s say you met Philip early in his story, before he gets serious about his faith. He’s out there playing the field and going to church now and then. What would you want to say to this guy?



Here’s some of what Philip said about his “playing” days: It was not really a competition between me and my friends. It was just a good feeling to know that I could [that is, that he could get a girl to have sex with him]. I thought that they [the girls] were going after the same thing I was, but they were after love and they were after relationships. I have hurt a lot of girls and they have told me this before. They automatically get attached, but with guys, it’s just another thing.



Do you think this is true? Do girls want “love and relationship” when guys want “just another thing.” Do relationships mean more to girls than to guys?



Leader: That was a loaded question. It’s a generalization that has a lot of evidence behind it, but there are many exceptions. Your students may talk about some girls they know who are “players” as much as Philip was, and some boys who long for relationships and get their hearts broken. Despite the exceptions, it’s an idea worth talking about.



If this is true, what can we do about that? Do girls need to be more careful about entering relationships? Do guys need to be more careful about what they promise? Does everyone need to talk more about what they’re really feeling?



Eric Mason talked about how, when you become a Christian, God takes “the player out of you.” What did he mean by that? Do you think it’s true?



Becoming a Christian doesn’t take away your sexual desire. We are still tempted, and sometimes we still sin. But a genuine Christian has a different way of seeing. The “player” sees a desirable person and says, “I want that.” That. It’s not really a person anymore. It’s a body, it’s an experience, it’s a conquest. The player uses the other person for his (or her) own pleasure. But as Christians, we see people through the lens of God’s love. That’s not an object I can use, it’s a person who is precious to God. So, whatever temptations I’m going through, I want to do what’s best for that person.

BIBLE JOURNEY
Leader: Distribute the Love Notes handout (example handout on page 8).



Turn to Romans 12. The Bible gives us great advice on how to develop good relationships—and we’re talking about all relationships, with friends and family as well as significant others. This passage gives us a dozen or more pointers. As we go through, jot down the specific ideas presented, along with any explanations you want to add.



Somebody read Romans 12:9–10 out loud.



“Love must be sincere.” Have you ever seen love that was not sincere? What does that look like?



How do you feel when you’re on the receiving end of insincere love?



You might say that the “Player” in the video was a master of insincere love. He would make girls think he loved them when he was just using them. And maybe you have friends who act all friendly one minute and put you down the next. Sincere love genuinely puts the other person first.



“Hate what is evil; cling to what is good.” How can we do this in relationships?



Say you’re with a group of friends who are normally really nice people. But then they start making fun of someone who’s not in their group, mocking them, maybe even bullying them. That’s “evil” behavior. It takes some courage to stand up against that, but you might have to find a way to affirm the “good” in your friends while hating the evil. “Hey, guys, we can do better than this. Are we so insecure that we have to tear other people down?”



How can you “honor” your friends, your family, even your boyfriend or girlfriend? What does that look like?



When we honor people, we show how valuable they are. When we honor them “above ourselves,” we’re putting their interests ahead of our own. When we “dishonor” someone, we treat them as if they’re not important, and certainly not as important as we are.



You might remember the commandment: “Honor your father and mother.” That means obeying them when you’re young, but it means far more as you grow older. It means acting as if they know more than you do, even if you’re sure they don’t. It means doing what they want rather than what you want. It doesn’t mean sneaking around, or doing things you know they wouldn’t like.



What if that sense of honor extended to your brothers and sisters? Not that you have to obey them, but what if you showed respect for them? What if you said good things about them rather than bad things?



And what if you treated your friends with honor, not trash-talking all the time, but sincerely saying complimentary things? And what if you treated your boyfriend or girlfriend with honor, not just trying to get your way, but truly seeking what’s best for them?



Somebody read verses 11–12.



What is zeal? (We might call it “good desire.” It’s a passion for good things, for God things.)



How could you show a zeal or spiritual fervor in your relationships with people?



It’s not necessarily preaching to everyone you meet, but it might come through in a desire for their spiritual growth. You might look for ways that God is working in their lives, and support them in their efforts to know God better.



Notice that we’re called to be “faithful in prayer.” Do you pray regularly for your friends, for your family? How does meaningful prayer affect the way you treat these people?



Somebody read verse 13.



How would you define hospitality? How can you show this to your friends?



The basic meaning of hospitality is opening your home to others, but that might be difficult if you always have to ask your parents for permission. So let’s see if we can expand that definition. Could hospitality involve “making people feel at home” wherever you are? At school, at church, going out with friends . . . what can you do to make everyone else feel like they belong?



And don’t miss the call to share with people in need. As we’re talking about relationships, could we consider those people who don’t have many friends? Certainly they are socially needy. Could you take the responsibility to reach out in friendship to those people that get rejected by others? That could be a scary thing—maybe you’ll get rejected too—but isn’t that what Jesus-followers do? And maybe that could become part of our identity as Christians, that we become friends of everybody—rich or poor, popular or not, good-looking or not-so-much.



Somebody read verses 14–16, and as you listen, keep jotting down the different points you hear. Think about how these things might apply to your relationships.



Creative Project



Leader: Break into groups of 4–7 people. The assignment is this. Each group is to choose one of the sentences or phrases from Romans 12:9–16 and create a scene to illustrate it. This scene could depict someone interacting with family, friends or a boyfriend/girlfriend. The group could write a dialogue involving two or more people, one of whom is showing this quality. Or they could write a narration and have actors mime the action. Or the whole thing could be a wordless mime of this kind of action occurring.

﻿

Give them 8–10 minutes (more or less as needed) to create these scenes, and then draw the groups together for showtime. Have each group present its scene. (Keep this fun, with no pressure to make it brilliant.) As time permits, talk about the scene afterward. What phrase was being depicted? What effect might that have on a relationship?

LAST WORD
In today’s video we saw the stories of a number of people who were deeply affected by troubled relationships. Sometimes love hurts. And sometimes the lack of love hurts. Love is never easy. Maybe that’s why the Bible says so much about it. Romans 12 is just one of many passages that tell us how to love others in a Christ-like way.



This is a series about identity, and you have a choice of what kind of person you want to be. Will you be twisted and turned every which way by the way others treat you in relationships? Will you fight back and try to get your way, using and manipulating those around you? Or will you let the love of Jesus drive you forward into healthy relationships, meaningful friendships, connections that honor everyone involved and bring glory to God?